protected String[] flatten(Options options, String[] arguments, boolean stopAtNonOption) {
    init();
    this.options = options;

    // an iterator for the command line tokens
    Iterator iter = Arrays.asList(arguments).iterator();

    // process each command line token
    while (iter.hasNext()) {
        // get the next command line token
        String token = (String) iter.next();

        // handle long option --foo or --foo=bar
        if (token.startsWith("--")) {
            if (token.indexOf('=') != -1) {
                tokens.add(token.substring(0, token.indexOf('=')));
                tokens.add(token.substring(token.indexOf('=') + 1, token.length()));
            } else {
                tokens.add(token);
            }
        }
        // other cases for single hyphen and options
        else {
            // logic for processing options and tokens
        }

        gobble(iter);
    }

    return (String[]) tokens.toArray(new String[tokens.size()]);
}
```

Based on the code snippet, the method `flatten` processes long options correctly by handling cases where the token starts with "--" and may have an '=' sign to split the option and its argument.

To address the issue described in the bug report where the `PosixParser` continues processing tokens after an unrecognized long option, you may need to review how the method `processOptionToken` or `burstToken` is handling such scenarios. It is likely that there might be an issue within these methods causing the unexpected behavior.

